Linux Server Administration:

Installed and configured Ubuntu Server in a virtual machine.
Created and managed Linux user accounts.
Practiced basic filesystem ownership and permissions.
Used systemctl to check whether services were running.
Used basic Linux commands to inspect the system.
Networking
Configured and checked the server's network connection.
Identified the server's IP address.
Tested Internet connectivity.
Connected to the server remotely from macOS using SSH.
Learned how DHCP provides an IP address to a machine.
Web Server

Installed and configured Nginx as a basic web server.
I verified that the Nginx service was running and accessed the server from a web browser over HTTP.

This helped me understand a simple client-server setup:
Web Browser │ │ HTTP ▼ Ubuntu Server │ ▼ Nginx



Basic Security:

Configured Ubuntu's UFW firewall and allowed only the services required for the lab, including SSH and HTTP.
I also practiced using separate user accounts and filesystem permissions rather than giving every user unrestricted access.

Monitoring & Troubleshooting:

Practiced basic Linux monitoring using tools for:

Memory usage
Disk usage
Running processes
System load
Service status

I also used Ubuntu's system logs to investigate warnings and Nginx service activity. An important part of the project was learning that system warnings do not automatically mean that a service is broken. I had to look at the context and verify whether the actual service was functioning.


What I Learned:

This project helped me move from mostly theoretical knowledge toward practical IT infrastructure work.

In particular, I gained a better understanding of:

How a Linux server operates inside a virtual machine
How SSH enables remote administration
How IP addresses and basic networking work in a server environment
How a web server such as Nginx provides services to clients
Why firewalls and network ports matter
How Linux users and file permissions control access
How system monitoring and logs can help with troubleshooting
